{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel, etc.</b> In Torat Kohanim they expounded: Israelites bring a sin-offering, and gentiles do not bring a sin-offering for the seven Noahide commandments. And it is difficult: whence would it occur to the mind to say that they should bring, such that it was necessary to exclude them? And if from vows and freewill offerings — what do vows and freewill offerings, which are not for atonement, have in common that you should say the same of a sin-offering? And as they said in Ḥullin (Ḥullin 5a) regarding the requirement of the exclusion of an apostate, that Scripture excludes him in the olah and in the chatat — because it was necessary to exclude an apostate from the olah, and we do not learn it from the exclusion of the chatat, because the chatat is for atonement, whereas the olah is not — this far. And from this, in a case where they were included in the olah, which is freewill offerings and vows, one may not learn the chatat, which is for atonement. And it appears to say that the intention is to guard lest the inclusion of ״נ׶פֶשׁ״ that the verse stated include gentiles as well; therefore it says <b>children of Israel</b> to exclude gentiles, and the inclusion of <b>נֶפֶשׁ</b> is to include converts." "And it is difficult: was it not possible for it to write neither ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ nor ״נֶפֶשׁ,״ and I would know that gentiles are excluded for the reason we have written, and converts bring — whence would it occur to the mind to exclude them? This is no difficulty, for do we not find that Scripture found it necessary to include them at the beginning of the parashah, as it is written (Leviticus 1:2) ״אָדָם,״ and they expounded there in Torat Kohanim to include converts — and if so, one might have thought that it included them only for the olah and not for the chatat; therefore it informs us. And if so, had there not been the exclusion effected by the extra word ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ there would have been grounds to say that converts were not required, for since they were included at the beginning of the parashah they were included for all the details enumerated throughout the parashah, and the inclusion effected by the extra word ״נֶפֶשׁ״ would come only to include gentiles, who bring a sin-offering. Therefore it says ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ to exclude gentiles. From this you learn that the word ״נֶפֶשׁ״ is nothing but to include converts — and the inclusion at the beginning of the parashah was not sufficient, both for the reason that the rank of chatat is superior to olah, and for the reason that there was an interruption in the subject matter and one does not learn from it; therefore it was necessary. And further it appears to say that it is not from the extra word ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ that he derives it, but from the plain sense of ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ he indicates that it is not gentiles — and since from the plain sense of ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ converts too would be excluded, therefore it says ״נֶפֶשׁ״ to include converts. And from now on, although ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ is needed for its own sake throughout the entire Torah, to declare to whom this commandment comes — automatically, gentiles are excluded." "<b>נֶפֶשׁ.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Torat Kohanim): to include converts. And had it been to include women, it should have said ״אָדָם כִּי יֶחֱטָא,״ for adam includes male and female, as it is written (Genesis 5:2) ״וַיִּקְרָא שְׁמָם אָדָם.״" "And by way of remez (allusion), it intends that inasmuch as the soul of a wicked person is diminished by virtue of the evil deed through the intermediary of the sin committed intentionally — and therefore the wicked person is called dead in his lifetime, as it is written (Ezekiel 18:32) ״בְּמוֹת הַמֵּת״ — for he has no soul. And this is also what it says (Proverbs 23:2) ״אִם בַּעַל נֶפֶשׁ אָתָּה.״ And Scripture informed him that the soul is diminished also in an inadvertent sin — the truth is that it is not diminished entirely but only partially — and by virtue of such a diminishment it is that HaShem said: when he brings an offering, and through this the soul draws near to its root and its light will shine as at the beginning. But in an intentional sin, where it is diminished entirely, the remedy of the offering is of no avail, for there is no soul in existence to bring near — until he repents and the Day of Atonement passes over him and he is revived, in the manner of what it says (Ezekiel 18:32) ״הָשִׁיבוּ וִחְיוּ.״"], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:04.604279484Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}